{
  "file": "crates/trust-but-verify/src/outline/language.rs",
  "item": "fn[whole_comment]",
  "citations": [
    {
      "role": "cite",
      "name": "habeas:coverage",
      "commentary": "This callback fills the Profile interface for every non-Rust profile.\n Those profiles all set hashes_comment to never, so Builder::encode skips\n comment_prose for every source input; plain text does not use the grammar\n encoder at all. Rust hashes doc comments through its separate doc_prose.\n The fallback therefore has no reachable behavior in the supported profiles."
    }
  ],
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The `habeas:coverage` citation on `crates/trust-but-verify/src/outline/language.rs#fn[whole_comment]` is justified, and the commentary agrees with the current source.\n\n- **Fenced commentary:** The explanation is in fenced citation commentary, as the built-in requires. It gives a substantive reason, that the fallback is unreachable, rather than saying it is hard to cover.\n- **Non-Rust profiles:** The css, markdown, python, toml, typescript, tsx and yaml profiles, and the plain-text profile in `outline/plain.rs`, all set `hashes_comment: never` and `comment_prose` to `whole_comment`. `language.rs#fn[never]` always returns false.\n- **Encoder:** The only caller of `comment_prose` is `outline/mod.rs#impl[Builder<'tree, 'source>]/fn[encode]`. It calls `comment_prose` only when `hashes_comment` is true, so the callback is skipped for those profiles.\n- **Plain text:** The plain-text profile has `language: None`, which is consistent with the claim that it does not use the grammar encoder.\n- **Rust:** The Rust profile uses `hashes_comment: is_doc_comment` and `comment_prose: doc_prose`, so it does not reach `whole_comment`.\n- **Custom profiles:** The custom test profile in `outline/tests.rs` is built from the Rust profile with `..rust::PROFILE`, so it does not reach `whole_comment` either.\n\nThe commentary limits its claim to the supported profiles, and I found no contradicting reachable path.",
